SLOG, or Slow Motion Log, is a video recording mode that captures footage at a higher frame rate than standard video, allowing for smooth slow-motion playback. It also records in a log profile, which provides a flat image with a wide dynamic range, ideal for post-production color grading.

When shooting in SLOG, the camera's sensor is exposed to light for a longer duration due to the increased frame rate. This can lead to overexposure, especially in bright conditions. To maintain proper exposure while keeping a wide aperture (for shallow depth of field) and a suitable shutter speed (to avoid motion blur), ND (Neutral Density) filters are used. ND filters reduce the amount of light entering the lens without affecting the color, allowing the camera to achieve the desired exposure settings in bright environments.

\boxed{SLOG is a slow-motion log recording mode that requires ND filters to manage exposure in bright conditions while maintaining a wide aperture and proper shutter speed.}

