GMT, or Greenwich Mean Time, is a time standard that is based on the solar time at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, London. It is the mean solar time at the IERS Reference Meridian (formerly called the Prime Meridian), which is by convention 0° longitude. GMT is significant because it serves as the reference point for all other time zones around the world. 

Local time zones are essentially offsets from GMT. Each time zone is defined by how many hours it is ahead of or behind GMT. For example, the image shows that West Hartford, Connecticut, is in the Eastern Standard Time (EST) zone, which is GMT-5. This means that when it is 12:00 noon GMT, it is 7:00 AM in West Hartford. During Daylight Saving Time (DST), the offset changes to GMT-4, so the time difference would be 8 hours behind GMT.

In summary, GMT is the baseline from which all other time zones are calculated, and local time zones are simply offsets from this baseline. \boxed{GMT is the reference point for all other time zones, and local time zones are offsets from GMT.}

